We were interested in the role of functional groups in these MOFs and their methane storage capacities, which motivated us to construct a series of highly porous MOFs that have well-separated functionalized linkers. Three functionalized MOF-905 (MOF-905-Me2, MOF-905- Naph, and $\mathsf{M O F}{\cdot}905{\cdot}\mathsf{N O}_{2},$ ) were obtained with the same synthetic method, using the acid forms of ditopic BDC-Me2, NDC, and BDC$\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ linkers instead of BDC (Scheme 1). Similar to MOF-905, structural determination of these functionalized MOFs by single crystal diffraction techniques was not successful. Instead, their structure and phase purity were confirmed by comparing their PXRD patterns by simulation from the modeled structures (SI, Sections S2 and S3). It should be noted that the diameter of larger cages for a series of functionalized MOF-905 ranges from 15.3 to $17.6\mathrm{\AA}$ (for MOF-905-Naph and $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2},}\end{array}$ respectively), while the diameter of smaller cages is still greater than that of expanded version, MOF-205 (Table 1).
Porosity of MOFs. Prior to the methane adsorption tests, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for all five MOFs were recorded at $77\mathrm{K}$ to confirm the presence of permanent porosity and to calculate their pore volume (Table 1). The guest-free (activated) materials were obtained by solvent exchange with chloroform followed by direct evacuation of pores under dynamic vacuum ( $4\times10^{-5}$ bar). Successful removal of guest molecules from the pore was also confirmed by thermogravimetric analysis (SI, Section S4). All examined MOFs displayed significant $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ uptake in the low pressure region $(P/P_{0}<0.05)$ and were saturated around $P/P_{0}=0.2$ (SI, Figures S12 to S16). The profiles of the isotherms are all classified as Type I and the adsorption of ${\bf N}_{2}$ was fully reversible, which is characteristic of permanent microporosity. Compared to MOF-905, the functionalized MOF-905 materials show slightly lower $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ uptake because the presence of extra functional groups reduces the pore volume. All of these materials exhibit high BET surface areas $\left(>3000~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ and large pore volumes (ca. $1.3~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\AA}$ , which are in good agreement with those calculated from their structural models. The BET (Langmuir) surface area, pore volume, crystal density, pore diameter are summarized along with other benchmark MOFs with the $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{-CO}_{2}\right)_{6}$ SBU in Table 1.
Low-Pressure Methane Isotherms and Adsorption Enthalpies. Low-pressure methane uptake isotherms for MOF-950, MOF-905, and functionalized MOF-905 were measured up to 1.1 bar at 273, 283, and $298\mathrm{K}$ (SI, Figures S17-S21). Methane uptakes in these MOFs increases linearly with pressure, with no saturation in uptake observed in the pressure region studied. Methane uptake for these new MOFs at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1.1 bar ranges from $8.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{'MOF}{\cdot}905{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ to $11.0~\mathsf{c m}^{3}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (MOF-905-Me2), and these are comparable to the methane uptake in
MOF-177 $\left(9.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ) under the same conditions (Table 1). Such moderate methane uptake capacity below 1.1 bar should be advantageous in achieving a larger working capacity for practical natural gas storage processes. It is known that low-pressure methane adsorption capacity correlates to the adsorption enthalpy rather than porosity of sorbent materials. Accordingly, the isosteric enthalpy of adsorption $(Q_{s\mathrm{t}})$ for methane was calculated based on methane isotherms collected at 273, 283, and $298\mathrm{K}.$
Figure S22 demonstrates the coverage dependencies of $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ calculated from fitting these data. MOF-950 shows slightly higher near-zero coverage $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ value than MOF-905 (11.9 and $11.7\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively); however, the profile of these $Q_{s\mathrm{t}}$ curves is nearly flat in the low coverage region. We note that these $Q_{s\mathrm{t}}$ values are comparable to those in MOF-5 $\left(12.3\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ ), MOF-177 $(9.9\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{\cdot1}})\mathrm{~,~}$ , and MOF-205 $\left(10.6\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ ), MOFs with $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{-CO}_{2}\right)_{6}$ SBUs; but less than those of Ni-MOF-74 $(21.4\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ), PCN-14 $\mathrm{(18.7~kJ~mol^{\cdot}l^{\cdot})}$ , and HKUST-1 $(17.0\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{\cdot1})$ , MOFs with open metal sites.9a Considering that the $Q_{s\mathrm{t}}$ values of MOFs with $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{-CO}_{2}\right)_{6}$ SBUs are not substantially influenced by the surface coverage of methane, it is presumed that primary methane adsorption sites should be located near the SBU.
Compared to MOF-905, three functionalized MOF-905 materials show lower $Q_{s\mathrm{t}}$ values of 10.3 $\left(-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\right)$ , 11.3 (-Naph), and 10.7 $\left(-\mathbf{NO}_{2}\right)$ $\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , although it is proposed that methyl functionalities can have a positive impact on the methane adsorption.25 Assuming that the primary methane adsorption sites are nearby the $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{-CO}_{2}\right)_{6}$ SBUs at ambient pressure, it is likely that the introduced methyl functionalities can block access of methane to the primary adsorption site rather than enhance the adsorbate-adsorbent interactions based on the functionalization of the benzene with electron donating groups. This is in contrast to the trend that Me and Cl functionalized $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs show higher $Q_{s t}$ .26 However, these MOFs have relatively small pore diameter (ca. 7 Å) such that the increased $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values can be attributed to the pore size effect.
High-Pressure Methane Storage. High-pressure excess methane isotherms (up to 80 bar) for MOF-950, MOF-905, and functionalized MOF-905 were recorded at $298\mathrm{K}$ on a volumetric adsorption analyzer to evaluate the storage performance of methane (Figure 3a). Methane uptake in these MOFs gradually increases with increasing pressure. Methane uptake below 20 bar is similar, while MOF-950 with the smallest pore diameter among $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF showed the highest methane uptake in the medium pressure range $\left(20{-}50\mathrm{bar}\right)$ . By increasing pressure further, excess uptake in each case is approaching saturation. The excess uptake at 80 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ is nearly proportional to
