the surface area (pore volume) of MOFs. MOF-905 showed the highest methane uptake of $310\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and this is followed by MOF-950 $(297\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , MOF-905-Me $(294\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{\mathrm{-1}})$ , MOF-905-Naph $289\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ), and $\mathsf{M O F}{\cdot}905{\cdot}\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ $(261~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{\bf~g}^{-1})$ ). Volumetric excess methane uptake can be calculated by multiplying the bulk density of materials. However, experimental bulk density cannot be the same as the theoretical one calculated from the crystal structure when the MOF sample is poorly activated or contains defect sites in its structure. Therefore, prior to unit conversion, we sought to estimate the bulk density of these MOFs using skeletal density ( $\dot{Q}_{\mathrm{skl}}=1/V_{\mathrm{skl}})$ and pore volume $(V_{\mathtt{P}})_{\mathtt{\Gamma}}$ , because these are experimentally determined using He pycnometry and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption, respectively:
$$
\mathrm{Experimentalbulk~density}=1/\big(1/d_{\mathrm{skl}}+V_{\mathrm{p}}\big)\qquad\mathrm{Eq.}
$$
As demonstrated in Table 1, the experimental bulk density for these MOFs was $5\mathrm{-}10\%$ lower compared to the theoretical crystal density, which is indicative of the presence of defects in the MOF crystals. The maximum excess methane uptake for MOF-905 in volumetric units is $167\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 80 bar and $298\mathrm{K},$ which outperforms MOF-950 (153 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ ) and functionalized MOF-905 (160, 151, and $144~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for -Naph, $\cdot\mathrm{Me}_{2},$ and - $\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ respectively).
To explore the potential as sorbent materials in their practical application, the total methane uptake in volumetric units is commonly used. Accordingly, the total uptake was calculated from the excess methane uptake using the following equation:
$$
N_{\mathrm{{total}}}=N_{\mathrm{{excess}}}+\rho_{\mathrm{{CH4}}}\times V_{\mathrm{{P}}}.
$$
As shown in Figure 3b, calculated total methane uptake monotonously increases with pressure, with no saturation behavior observed. The total volumetric methane uptake for MOF-950, MOF-905, and functionalized MOF-905 at 80 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ are ranging from 203 $(\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}905{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2})$ ) to $228\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ (MOF-905). Remarkably, methane uptake in MOF-905 at 80 bar is 2.7 times larger than bulk methane density at the same temperature and pressure. This value is also $10\%$
